  longDescription:
    "1830: THE GAME OF RAILROADS AND ROBBER BARONS is a SEMINAL 1986 STRATEGIC BOARD GAME designed by FRANCIS TRESHAM (best known for creating the 1980 game CIVILIZATION which inspired SID MEIER's video game) and PUBLISHED BY AVALON HILL — the game that ESTABLISHED THE 18xx GENRE of railroad-and-stock-manipulation board games. HISTORY: Tresham published 1829 (covering British railways) in 1974 as part of his HARTLAND TREFOIL games line — this was the FIRST 'STOCK-AND-RAILROAD' game. AVALON HILL licensed the system + Tresham designed 1830 (covering 1830s NORTHEAST USA railroads) which released 1986 to critical acclaim + became one of the most influential Euro-style strategic games ever made. The '18xx' NAME comes from the year in the title: 1830, 1829, 1856, 1870, 1889, etc. — the genre now includes 50+ VARIANTS worldwide covering railroads of specific historical periods + regions (British, American, German, French, Japanese, etc.). GAME OVERVIEW: 3-7 PLAYERS play as ROBBER BARONS in 1830s NORTHEAST USA, seeking to accumulate the most PERSONAL WEALTH ($) through strategic manipulation of RAILROAD COMPANIES. GAMEPLAY ALTERNATES between TWO PHASES: (1) STOCK ROUNDS — players BUY + SELL SHARES of railroad companies on the STOCK MARKET (tracked on a 2D price chart); becoming the PRESIDENT of a company (owning 20% first) triggers COMPANY OPERATIONS. (2) OPERATING ROUNDS — companies (in stock-price order) BUILD TRACK on the HEX MAP (buying + placing tile pieces representing rails + curves + junctions), LAY TOKENS in cities, OPERATE TRAINS (buying trains of varying capacity + running them along routes for revenue), and PAY DIVIDENDS TO SHAREHOLDERS (or withhold + build up company treasury). KEY MECHANICS: TRAIN RUSTING (older trains become obsolete when new-generation trains are bought — a signature 18xx mechanic that forces companies to constantly reinvest), SHORT-SELLING via presidency dumps, BANKRUPTCY (players eliminated if unable to pay obligations), CORPORATE SABOTAGE via track placement blocking opponents' routes. WINNER: player with the MOST TOTAL PERSONAL WEALTH (cash + stock at final prices) at game end. GAME LENGTH: 4-6 HOURS (long by casual standards but standard for 18xx). WEIGHT: BoardGameGeek weight ~4.5/5 — among the HEAVIEST hobby board games; extremely high skill ceiling + steep learning curve. INFLUENCE: 1830 established the 18XX GENRE + inspired dozens of designers (LONNY ORGLER, HELMUT OHLEY, IAN WILSON, JOHN HARRES, etc.). Modern 18xx variants include 1856, 1870, 1889 Japan, 1817 (a modern take with corporation mergers), 18Chesapeake (beginner-friendly), 18India, etc. RANKINGS: BoardGameGeek Top 100 (peaked around #100-150 for decades); regarded by hobbyists as THE FINEST STOCK-MARKET/BUSINESS SIMULATION IN BOARD GAMES + a foundational text of strategic gaming. AVAILABILITY: MAYFAIR GAMES republished 2011 (updated art + components); LOOKOUT SPIELE re-issued 2019; digital versions exist (18XX.games online + Steam apps). Playing physically requires 2-D card table with room for track hex map + stock chart + player mats. Considered a CLASSIC + INITIATION into serious hobby board gaming.",
